   246	  (A1.2.1); the new conjunct is part of it. The P4 partition applies
   247	  inside it: performed-and-failed → `INVALID`; unperformable (headers
   248	  unavailable) → `UNVERIFIABLE`; neither is promotable.
   249	
   250	---
   251	
   252	## A2.3 The re-issue loop terminates: bounded attempts, fail-closed refusal
   253	
   254	As registered, the P5 corollary's re-issue rule was an unbounded loop, and
   255	its dominant triggers — fee spikes, congestion, calendar outage — are
   256	*correlated across attempts*: retrying under the same conditions is not a
   257	fresh draw, so "re-issue until it works" can livelock. Fail-closed means
   258	issuance must be able to end in refusal, not only in success.
   259	
   260	Amended: issuance makes at most **N attempts**. The expiry clock is the
   261	**wall clock** (A2.1 clock roles): an attempt expires when wall time
   262	passes `declared + δ + S`, except that timely-latched eligibility
   263	excludes expiry — eligible/finalizing, shipped, and expired/refused are
   264	mutually exclusive states (construction obligation, registered with the
   265	ruling). Exhausting them obligates
   266	the implementation to terminate issuance in an **explicit refusal, durably
   267	recorded; reporting is a delivery obligation to be registered in
   268	Amendment 3** — a first-class protocol outcome, not an error path. N is a
   269	protocol constant ratified at Band 0 exit alongside δ, ε, k, and S
   270	(working default **N = 3**; per the A2.1 clock-roles ruling each
   271	attempt's lifecycle is wall-governed through δ + S, so N attempts give
   272	a **nominal lifecycle budget** of N × (δ + S) — twelve days at the
   273	working defaults δ = 72 h, S = 24 h — a nominal budget, not a proven
   274	wall-clock bound on the process; see the model note).
   275	[**Note:** refusal as a separate delivery obligation added 2026/07/21 per
   276	review process/response.]
   277	
   278	Model note: P5c's `MaxAttempts` is hereby promoted from a state-space
   279	bound to protocol semantics. The refusal state is modeled by **atomic
   280	entry** (the transition expiring the final attempt's window records the
   281	refusal in the same step; checked, with a broken companion red on
   282	exactly the silent-deadlock invariant among the checked set — review
   283	archived in
   284	`docs/reviews/2026-07-20-codex-p5c-refusal-review.md`). Scope of proof
   285	versus contract, per that review: the model proves the *conditional
   286	safety half* — the refusal state is entered atomically and latches; it
   287	does not prove the crossing occurs (no fairness is assumed), so
   288	**termination is a contract obligation on the implementation, not a
   289	proven liveness property**. The unbounded "eventually ships" claim
   290	likewise remains explicitly unproven. Storage durability, retrievability,
   291	and reporting of the refusal record are implementation/handoff
   292	obligations to be registered in Amendment 3 (planned, not yet in force).
   293	[**Note:** model-note scope split added 2026/07/21, same review cycle.]
   294	
   295	---
   296	
   297	## A2.4 Anchor identity and declared-time confinement
   298	
   299	- **A receipt's anchor is the proof it ships with.** A shipped receipt
   300	  contains exactly one anchor proof (the OTS proof over its canonical
   301	  bytes) together with the anchor transaction's id, which serves as the
   302	  **identity handle** binding the receipt to its chain transaction. (An
   303	  identity/coherence handle, not by itself a retrieval mechanism:
   304	  txid → transaction *availability* requires chain access — a node,
   305	  index, or archive — which is an implementation obligation under the
   306	  evidence discipline, joining A2.2's; the hash argument below covers
   307	  identity, not availability.) Verification evaluates the
   308	  shipped proof and no other; an anchor not shipped inside a receipt
   309	  confers **no validity, no priority, and no claimed anchor identity**
   310	  in verification or dispute — in particular, a discarded attempt's
   311	  transaction confirming later confers nothing toward any of the three.
   312	  (This confines *protocol standing*; it does not render such artifacts
   313	  inadmissible as *evidence* — e.g., of issuer conduct — in dispute
   314	  processes. The stand-alone anchor-standing property is Amendment 3's
   315	  to register; this clause is written to underdetermine it, not
   316	  pre-empt it.) The handle
   317	  requires no new assumption: a txid is the double-SHA-256 of the
   318	  transaction, so txid → transaction → committed bytes rests on the
   319	  SHA-256 resistance already cited in Layer 2, and the OTS proof's merkle
   320	  path binds the anchored bytes to the block independently of the handle.
   321	  (The txid necessarily lives in the bundle outside the signed bytes — the
   322	  anchor is computed over those bytes, so the txid exists only after them;
   323	  the wrapper discipline of P7 makes the containment tamper-evident.)
   324	- **Declared times are existed-by claims, and only that.**
   325	  `declared_issue_time` asserts the artifact existed by that time;
   326	  re-declaration on re-issue moves it forward, which weakens and never
   327	  falsifies. **No verifier policy, and no downstream marketplace rule, may
   328	  order competing receipts by declared time** (first-to-file semantics):
   329	  the re-issue mechanism necessarily gives the issuer discretion over
   330	  declared times within the retry schedule, and priority ordering would
   331	  convert that discretion into a manipulation surface. This confinement is
   332	  a design commitment in the P6 monotonicity style — assumed by the model,
   333	  enforced by policy, deliberately not filed under Layer 2.
   334	
   335	---
   336	
   337	## A2.5 New model obligations
   338	
   339	- **Verifier-side family (P5/P6):** add the `confirmed_at` observable and
   340	  the A2.2 conjunct under the existing verifier-owns-tolerances
   341	  discipline; a broken companion omitting the conjunct must accept the
   342	  the chain-late subclass — witnessing exactly the gap A2.0 records.
   343	  Standing working rules apply (broken companions, `_Sanity` vacuity
   344	  witnesses for every defined-predicate antecedent).
   345	- **P5c:** the header's semantic-fork paragraph resolves to
   346	  ratified-strict in the A2.1 chain-time form; `MaxAttempts` semantics
   347	  per A2.3; `DepthK = k − 1` convention per A2.1.
   348	- **Conformance vectors (A1.7):** extracted traces must include the new
   349	  conjunct's reject case (late-burial artifact → `INVALID`) and its
   350	  unperformable case (missing headers → `UNVERIFIABLE`).
   351	- **Clock-roles analysis (discharged with the ruling, 2026-07-21):** the
   352	  slack companions `P5cP5P6_BridgeSlack.tla` / `_Latch.tla` check the
   353	  conditional opportunity guarantee (B − C ≤ S ⇒ timely opportunity at
   354	  eligibility; residue witnessed non-empty below the bound; cutoff
   355	  exercised; iff status = argument plus bounded check). The committed
   356	  bridge **registers the absence** of a lifecycle guard in its `Ship` —
   357	  it checks the chain-evidence seam only. Predictions-first record and
   358	  outcomes: `docs/reviews/2026-07-21-claude-predictions-slack-bench.md`;
   359	  ruling archive: `docs/reviews/2026-07-21-clock-precedence-ruling.md`.
   360	
   361	---
   362	
   363	## A2.6 What changed, and what did not
   364	
   365	This amendment **strengthens** P5 (a third strict conjunct), **adds**
   366	obligations (header evidence and authentication for stateless
   367	confirmation checking, the attempt bound with recorded refusal —
   368	reporting to be registered in Amendment 3, per A2.3 — the
   369	anchor-identity rule, the declared-time confinement commitment, new
   370	conformance-vector cases), **registers** a correction that narrows
   371	what the strict rule may be claimed to protect, **rules** the
   372	clock-roles question (chain time governs evidence, wall time governs
   373	the attempt lifecycle with operational slack S — a new constant
   374	joining δ, ε, k, N at Band 0 exit; the opportunity guarantee is
   375	conditional on the observable lag, and no hard backward-lag assumption
   376	is registered), and **withdraws** the two-receipts closure claim,
   377	re-registering it as an Amendment 3 obligation.
   378	
   379	Stated in A1.1's terms, exactly: no A1.2 property is weakened; no proof
   380	is demoted to an assumption; no declared A1.3 adversary capability is
   381	excluded; the construction remains dual-signature plus temporal anchor.
   382	The A1.1 falsifier is not triggered. In ordinary language, behavior
   383	does change, and this amendment does not pretend otherwise: the
   384	permissive reading's acceptances are excluded by ratifying strict, a
   385	refusal outcome and attempt bound now exist, exactly one shipped anchor
   386	carries protocol standing, and declared-time priority policies are
   387	prohibited — all registered strengthenings or confinements. One
   388	acknowledged cost: the new evidence obligation adds an `UNVERIFIABLE`
   389	path (headers unavailable) where pre-A2 verification could return a
   390	verdict — an availability cost of the strengthened check, carried by
   391	the P4 partition, not a weakening of any registered property. The
   392	bands, hypotheses, hard invariants, and division of labor are
   393	unchanged.

