docs/reviews/amendment-9/codex.log:9274:+   The lower comparison is not-before ≤ declared time, not not-before ≤ T. Let not-before = declared time = 12 October 12:00Z, T = 12 October 11:00Z, and the designated confirmation timestamp = 12:00Z. Both issuance-window comparisons and all three temporal conjuncts hold at ε = 24 h, while T is outside the window. Non-monotonic/inexact chain time is expressly permitted. A9 also makes not-before optional, so there need not be a lower issuance boundary at all.
docs/reviews/amendment-9/codex.log:9282:+   A2.1 explicitly says consensus supplies no finite bound on B − C and separates observation delay, clock error and backward timestamp skew. Neither ε = 24 h nor S = 24 h is a universal bound on how far true inclusion time can exceed a header timestamp. The predicate constrains chain timestamps against a declared time; it does not authenticate the thief’s declared time against UTC. Thus “no receipt passes with T > not-after” follows directly, but “no mint passes after true not-after plus the A2.1 skew bound” does not follow from these sources. ENCODING-V1 repeats the same unsupported inference, so agreement between the two documents is not independent evidence.
docs/reviews/amendment-9/codex.log:9284:+   The 96 h stop is larger than δ = 72 h, correcting the earlier 48 h choice. But δ already includes timestamp skew in A1, and is a declared-time-to-chain-time tolerance, not “the longest registered gap from signing to anchor” in wall time. Adding 24 h is an owner-selected operational reserve. It guarantees neither completion nor shipping by retirement; the retained no-ship/reissue rule supplies the safe failure behavior.
