docs/reviews/issuer/DISPOSITION.md:50:| CF-02 | Med | Writer fencing by lease: every writer runs inside a Lambda invocation that saw the request ACCEPTED, so no write lands later than `WRITE_LEASE_S` (900 s, the Lambda maximum) after the end was first seen. The obligation records `fence_until` on its first pass; until then each poll deletes again, and only a listing after it discharges the obligation. Covers writes that commit while their caller sees an error. | `test_CF02_upload_that_commits_after_the_end_is_still_deleted`, `test_CF02_signature_written_after_termination_is_still_deleted`, `test_CF02_write_lease_covers_every_lambda_timeout` |
docs/reviews/issuer/DISPOSITION.md:68:| CF2-01 | High | A clock check cannot bound when a write commits; the writer lease can, and it is the assumption CF-02 already rests on. `api.settle` confirms only when the decision is at least `WRITE_LEASE_S` before the deadline, so the confirmation commits before the deadline if AWS commits within the lease (stated, not established: CF3-01). Inside the last lease it leaves the request unconfirmed: it reads as ACCEPTED with its `fail_after`, and at the deadline it is FAILED. `_cleanup_one` treats an unconfirmed request as not ended, so it is cleaned up normally once settled. Cost: a request that finishes in the last 15 minutes of its 24 hours fails (S4 states about 3 hours as usual). The DESIGN.md CF-01 bullet's "no timing assumption is needed" was wrong and is corrected. | `test_CF2_01_confirmation_that_commits_late_never_reads_as_complete` (Codex's reproduction), `test_CF2_01_inside_the_last_lease_reads_as_accepted_until_the_deadline`, `test_CF2_01_a_confirmation_decided_before_the_last_lease_commits_before_the_deadline` (guards against an over-strict margin; it passes on the old code too); `test_CF01_completion_seen_before_the_deadline_never_turns_into_failed` moved to before the last lease |
docs/reviews/issuer/DISPOSITION.md:69:| CF2-02 | Med | `api.receipt` reads the record first and then the clock, immediately before minting. `api.status` had the same order (clock, then record) and is changed the same way. | `test_CF2_02_no_url_is_minted_after_expiry_even_if_the_record_read_is_slow` (Codex's reproduction, counting `presign` calls) |
docs/reviews/issuer/DISPOSITION.md:82:| CF3-01 | High | **Stated as a limit, with the client side bounded.** No client check can bound when AWS commits a request already sent. The AWS clients now get explicit timeouts and retries: about 3 minutes worst case, kept under a third of the lease by a test. The remaining assumption is recorded as the service clock tolerance (`docs/decisions/2026-10-27-service-clock-tolerance.md`): service-level transitions may become visible up to 15 minutes after the time they record. No receipt guarantee depends on these times. DESIGN.md and this file now call the CF2-01 bound conditional. | `test_CF3_01_aws_client_time_is_bounded_well_inside_the_writer_lease` |
docs/reviews/issuer/CODEX-CONFIRM-3.md:1:**CF2-01 remains conditional, not established closed. CF2-02’s record-read race is closed.** The sweep found additional instances and a retention-timestamp problem.
docs/reviews/issuer/CODEX-CONFIRM-3.md:9:| CF2-01 | The supplied deadline−1 reproduction is closed: no confirmation is attempted inside the final lease. However, the claim that Lambda termination bounds remote commit time remains unsupported. Closure requires an explicit remote-write bound, not merely a Lambda timeout. |
docs/reviews/issuer/CODEX-CONFIRM-3.md:10:| CF2-02 | Closed for the reported storage-read race. Both `status` and `receipt` now read the record before sampling time; `status` also refreshes time after settlement. A separate potential blocking boundary remains inside AWS presigning, described below. |
docs/reviews/issuer/CODEX-CONFIRM-3.md:35:   Suggested fix: establish and explicitly accept the remote commit assumption, or redesign the authoritative completion event and visibility protocol so correctness does not require it. Explicit client timeouts and retry budgets are useful controls, but alone do not discharge this assumption. This is CF2-01’s remaining gap, not a wholly separate regression.
